static const uint8_t kspl_embed_data_kspl_ksplc_tools_explain_diagnostics_spec[] =
    "# The ksplc diagnostics specification\n"
    "\n"
    "This document lists every diagnostic code `ksplc` reports: what each points at, and how to fix i"
    "t. **It is the text `ksplc explain <code>` prints**, embedded in the compiler, so the explanatio"
    "n is at hand where the diagnostic appeared. How a code is switched on, off or promoted to an err"
    "or, and the four weights, are \"5. Static analysis and the linter (ksplc lint)\" in [`SPEC-tools"
    ".md`](SPEC-tools.md).\n"
    "\n"
    "---\n"
    "\n"
    "## 1. Diagnostic codes, and the errors that have none\n"
    "\n"
    "A diagnostic code's letter gives the weight (\"5.2 The diagnostics' four weights\" in [`SPEC-too"
    "ls.md`](SPEC-tools.md#52-the-diagnostics-four-weights)), and each code is explained in [2](#2-th"
    "e-list-of-warnings-w) / [3](#3-the-list-of-information-diagnostics-i) / [4](#4-the-list-of-hints"
    "-h).\n"
    "\n"
    "**`ksplc explain <code>` prints that code's section** (the code in either case), so the explanat"
    "ion is available where the diagnostic appeared. **`ksplc explain` with no code lists every code*"
    "*, one line each with its title, in this document's order, and marks the codes off by default. *"
    "*A code's start lists the codes it begins**: `ksplc explain W` lists the warnings, `ksplc explai"
    "n I03` the codes from I0301 on. A compile or lint that printed a coded diagnostic names the comm"
    "and under its summary. An error, having no code, has no section: its message explains itself.\n"
    "\n"
    "**Seven shapes are errors with no code**, so no setting can silence them (only a coded diagnosti"
    "c has a level, \"5.2 The diagnostics' four weights\" in [`SPEC-tools.md`](SPEC-tools.md#52-the-d"
    "iagnostics-four-weights)). The first four silently change what runs. The fifth is written but do"
    "es nothing. The last two are a second way of writing what the notation already says:\n"
    "\n"
    "| The shape | What goes wrong |\n"
    "| :-- | :-- |\n"
    "| a division or remainder by `0` | changes what runs |\n"
    "| a bit shift by the left side's type width or more, or by a negative amount | changes what runs"
    " |\n"
    "| an integer literal outside its range (`U8 = 300` and the like) | changes what runs |\n"
    "| a line in the indentation notation whose column matches no block | the reader and the compiler"
    " would disagree. `ksplc fmt` rebuilds the columns from the tree, so no formatted source meets it"
    " |\n"
    "| an `#export` on a `priv` | written but does nothing: `priv` makes the generated C `static`, so"
    " the exported name still links to nothing outside (the attribute-combination check refuses it) |"
    "\n"
    "| a `;` at the end of a statement's line in the indentation notation | a second way of writing t"
    "he line's end, which already ends the statement. `ksplc fmt` drops it |\n"
    "| a `;` right after the `}` of a block written on one line, where that block ends its statement "
    "(`if c { f() }; g()`) | a second way of writing that end, which the `}` already made. A block in"
    "side a value (`x: = f() catch { 0 }; g(x)`) ends no statement, and its `;` stays |\n"
    "\n"
    "The language rules are \"2.3 Literals\" and \"6.8 Integer arithmetic: folding, overflow, divisio"
    "n, shifts\" in [`../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md`](../../docs/SPEC-programming-language."
    "md), and for `#export`, \"11.3 Exposing a KSPL function to C (`#export`)\" in the same document."
    "\n"
    "\n"
    "## 2. The list of warnings (W)\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0101] An out-of-bounds array access\n"
    "A constant index on a fixed-length array lies outside it: past its end (`a[5]` on an `I32[3]`) o"
    "r below 0 (`a[-1]`).\n"
    "* **Fix**: correct the index, or it panics (stops the program with a message) at run time.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0102] A side effect lost in an array length\n"
    "An array's `.len` is evaluated at compile time, so its subject expression never runs. A side eff"
    "ect there (a function call, say) is discarded.\n"
    "* **Fix**: move the side effect into its own statement, keep its result in a variable, and take "
    "`.len` from that.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0103] A digit-accumulating loop with no ceiling check\n"
    "A loop builds a number digit by digit and never checks that it stays under a limit, so a long in"
    "put overflows it. The warning fires where a `for` accumulates in one of these two patterns, and "
    "no `if` in that loop tests the accumulated variable (a ceiling check):\n"
    "1. `x = x * <an integer literal> + <an expression>` (on signed integers)\n"
    "2. `x *= <an expression>` (a compound multiplication; on unsigned integers and `Sz` as well)\n"
    "* **Fix**: compare the variable against the permitted ceiling around the accumulation, and treat"
    " exceeding it as an error (see `parse_number` in `std/json.kspl` and `content_length` in `std/js"
    "onrpc.kspl`).\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0104] A pointer from an arena handed to a free\n"
    "Memory from an arena (a block that hands out pieces and is freed all at once) is freed piece by "
    "piece. A pointer from `arena.alloc()` / `arena.alloc_as<|T|>()` / `arena.create<|T|>()` is passe"
    "d to `mem.dealloc()` (the free function that releases a byte run).\n"
    "* **Only the free function counts**: the method form (`x.free()`), which releases `x`'s contents"
    " rather than `x`'s address, is not reported.\n"
    "* **Only a binding that is never reassigned is tracked** (a `$p` reassigned to another allocatio"
    "n is not).\n"
    "* The verdict goes by the receiver's type (`std.mem.Arena` itself), so another type with a metho"
    "d of the same name is not caught.\n"
    "* **Fix**: do not free arena memory piece by piece. Free the whole arena with `arena.free()`, or"
    " roll back with `arena.save()` / `arena.restore()` to discard part of it early.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0105] A borrow used outside the lock\n"
    "A value protected by a lock is reached outside the lock. `thread.Mutex<|T|>.lock()` returns a mu"
    "table pointer (a borrow) to the protected value. The borrow is either:\n"
    "* (A) used after the matching `unlock()` (access without the lock, a data race: two threads can "
    "change the value at once), or\n"
    "* (B) `return`ed to the caller.\n"
    "\n"
    "It walks one function's straight-line code. A user-defined guard type of the same shape is caugh"
    "t too, and a simple alias (`q: = p;`) inherits the tracking. The tracking stops at a branch, a l"
    "oop or a nested block, so it catches the typical mistake rather than guaranteeing soundness.\n"
    "* **Fix**: for (A), use the borrow before the `unlock()`, or `lock()` again just before the use."
    " For (B), copy out the value needed while holding the lock, and return the copy.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0106] A lock taken and not released\n"
    "After `thread.Mutex<|T|>.lock()`, neither an `unlock()` nor a `defer` releasing it is found befo"
    "re the scope is left. Another thread waiting for the lock then waits forever. It uses [W0105]'s "
    "straight-line walk, with the same limits. A registered `defer g.unlock()` releases on every exit"
    ", so it is not reported.\n"
    "* **Fix**: call `g.unlock()` when done, or, more safely, `defer g.unlock()` right after locking,"
    " which releases on every exit, `return`, `throw` and `break` included.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0107] A method that frees outside a release contract\n"
    "A method frees its object, but the compiler cannot tell, so using the object afterwards goes unr"
    "eported. The use-after-free check reads \"freed\" only when the receiver's type conforms to `Man"
    "ual_free` or `Drop` and the method called is `free`. So a `pub` method that frees its receiver o"
    "utside that contract lets `x.close(); x.read();` compile with zero diagnostics. It is reported w"
    "here the name differs (one of `close` / `destroy` / `deinit` / `dispose` / `shutdown` / `delete`"
    " / `teardown`), or where it does not conform (the name is `free`, but the type has no `impl X: M"
    "anual_free {}`).\n"
    "* **Fix**: where it frees, name it `free` and add `impl X: Manual_free {}`. `Manual_free`'s `fre"
    "e` returns no failure, so move a clean-up whose result must be checked (a flush, say) into a sep"
    "arate method. An operation that does not free the receiver, or that takes an argument (`delete(k"
    "ey)`), is out of scope.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0108] A bit operation's operand is compared without brackets\n"
    "The result of `&` / `|` / `^` is compared without brackets, as in `a & b == c`. KSPL binds all t"
    "hree more tightly than comparison, so it reads `(a & b) == c`. **C reads it the other way** (`a "
    "& (b == c)`), and neither the types nor a run-time check catch it. Where each operator stands is"
    " the table in \"6.1 The operator list and precedence\" in [`../../docs/SPEC-programming-language"
    ".md`](../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md#61-the-operator-list-and-precedence).\n"
    "* **Fix**: bracket the intended grouping (`(a & b) == c`). [H0301]'s \"unneeded brackets\" does "
    "not look at binary operations, so both can be followed.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0109] An early exit before the `defer` that frees a `Manual_free` value\n"
    "A value that must be freed can leave the function before its `defer` is reached, so it leaks. Th"
    "e `defer` is the statement that frees it at the end of the block. A `!`, `return`, `throw`, or a"
    " `break` / `continue` out of the block stands between binding a value whose type conforms to `Ma"
    "nual_free` and the `defer name.free()` later in the same block. That path leaves before the `def"
    "er` is registered, so the value is never freed. The finding sits on the exit and names both line"
    "s.\n"
    "Automatic owners receive no such warning: their cleanup already covers every exit.\n"
    "* **Only a later `defer` in the same block starts the question.** A value freed by hand, passed "
    "on or kept has another shape. It is not read as a leak.\n"
    "* **Not reported**:\n"
    "  * a `return` that returns the value itself (the caller owns it then);\n"
    "  * a statement freeing it by hand on the exiting path (`if bad { x.free(); return }`);\n"
    "  * an exit inside a closure, which leaves only the closure.\n"
    "* **Fix**: put the `defer` on the line right after the binding, so it runs on every exit. A type"
    " that allocates only on its first write (`Str_builder.new()`) leaks nothing on that path, but mo"
    "ving the `defer` up costs nothing there either.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0201] Unreachable code\n"
    "Code that never runs, because it follows a `return`, a `throw`, a `for` that never ends (no cond"
    "ition, and no `break` leaves it) or the like. What follows a labelled `switch`, `if` or block th"
    "at a `break` to its label leaves is reachable, whatever its branches end in.\n"
    "* **Fix**: delete it. A function returning a value needs no `return` after a `for` that never en"
    "ds (\"Loops\" in [`../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md`](../../docs/SPEC-programming-languag"
    "e.md#72-loops)).\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0202] An unused expression with no side effect\n"
    "An expression is evaluated, but its result is not stored. It holds no side effect (a function ca"
    "ll, an assignment), so it does nothing. Examples are a plain `1 + 1;` and a bare variable refere"
    "nce.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0203] A meaningless self-assignment\n"
    "A variable is assigned its own value, as in `a = a;`.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0204] A meaningless comparison\n"
    "A variable is compared with itself, as in `a == a` or `b <= b`, so the result is fixed.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0205] A constant condition\n"
    "An `if` or `for` condition is a boolean literal, as in `if true` / `if false` or `for true` / `f"
    "or false`.\n"
    "* **Fix**: drop the condition from `for true`, leaving a bare `for` that goes round until someth"
    "ing leaves it, and delete `for false` (it never runs).\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0206] An unused private symbol\n"
    "A `priv` function, struct or the like is never referred to within its file.\n"
    "* **Fix**: delete it, or, where it is kept on purpose for later, start its name with `_`.\n"
    "* **Every file the compile reads is checked**, an imported one too. A `priv` can be named only i"
    "n its own file, and every file is read whole. So a lint of one entry point finds a dead helper i"
    "n any file it imports.\n"
    "* **Not reported**:\n"
    "  * a helper that only a generic's body names, although no caller instantiates the generic;\n"
    "  * a method that a conformance (`impl T: Trait`) requires, called or not. Removed, the `impl` n"
    "o longer conforms.\n"
    "* **A `#cfg` leaves out what one build does not hold.** A helper that only a hosted function cal"
    "ls is unused in a freestanding build. Put the same `#cfg` on the helper.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0207] A local variable or parameter nobody reads\n"
    "A local variable or a parameter is **never read**. Reading counts, not the name appearing. A fix"
    "ed-length array's `.len` folds to a constant at translation time. So an `a: Sz[3] = ::{ 1, 2, 3 "
    "};` used only through `a.len` fires it: nobody reads the elements, only the folded value remains"
    ", and the array is dead.\n"
    "* **Fix**: delete the variable, or, where a trait's constraint requires the parameter, start its"
    " name with `_` (`_x`) to ignore it explicitly. Where only the length is needed, declare a `const"
    "` rather than an array.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0208] A variable assigned but never read\n"
    "A value is assigned to a variable and never read afterwards.\n"
    "* **Fix**: where it is deliberate, start the name with `_`.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0209] A `//<` continuation that continues nothing\n"
    "A `//<` line meant to continue a description has nothing to continue, so its text is shown nowhe"
    "re. A line holding only a `//<` and its text continues the `//<` above it, as part of that decla"
    "ration's summary. A run must be contiguous: a blank line or a plain `//` comment ends it. Where "
    "the line above has no `//<` (one of those stands between, or nothing is above), there is no run "
    "to join. The text then reaches neither the editor's hover nor the record. The mark is written an"
    "d the sentence is not read. That is the \"writable, and with no effect\" shape that is refused.\n"
    "* **Fix**: put the `//<` the run continues on the line above (behind the declaration or the memb"
    "er itself). Where the text is not that declaration's documentation, make it a plain `//` comment"
    ".\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0210] A `//<` anchor with nothing continuing it\n"
    "A `//<` behind a declaration can carry no text and leave the whole description to the `//<` line"
    "s below it. This is the anchor shape defined in [2.1 of `SPEC-programming-language.md`](../../do"
    "cs/SPEC-programming-language.md#21-comments). Where no such line follows, the run is empty and t"
    "he entry is published with no description. It mirrors [W0209]: there the sentence has no mark, h"
    "ere the mark has no sentence.\n"
    "* **Fix**: write the description on `//<` lines under the mark, or behind the mark where it fits"
    " the line. Where there is really nothing to say, remove the `//<`.\n"
    "* **It is reached by deleting**: remove the continuations and the anchor is left with nothing un"
    "der it.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0211] A name a comment points at leads nowhere\n"
    "A comment names, in backticks, something the code does not hold, such as a method under its old "
    "name. A dotted name in backticks in a comment (`` `Map.entry_list` ``, `` `io.out()` ``) is read"
    " as the code beside the comment would read it. The head is resolved through the file's own decla"
    "rations, its imports and the prelude, a parameter or local of the enclosing function first. Each"
    " later part is read as a member of the one before. Where the owner is found but lacks the member"
    " (a method renamed, a field moved, a function made a method), the pointer leads nowhere. A reade"
    "r finds nothing, and a model believes the name exists.\n"
    "* **Fix**: point at what exists.\n"
    "  * Another file can add a method to the type while that file is not in the compilation. Tie suc"
    "h a method to its file (`` `List.order_by` in `std/seq` ``); the document check reads it instead"
    " (`make test-docref`).\n"
    "  * Introduce a name another product owns as its own (\"Python's `` `json.dumps` ``\").\n"
    "* **Not reported**:\n"
    "  * a name whose head is a parameter or local of the enclosing function, or unknown to the file "
    "(a plain word);\n"
    "  * a name introduced as another product's or tied to its file;\n"
    "  * a name `#cfg` left out of this compilation but declared for another configuration;\n"
    "  * a name holding anything but dotted parts and call brackets (`` `io.out().s(\"x\")` `` is a c"
    "ode fragment).\n"
    "\n"
    "  A name whose last part is a file extension and names nothing (`` `edit.kspl` ``) is read as a "
    "file.\n"
    "* **The scope is the compilation's**: members are looked up across every file compiled together."
    " So the lint gate, which compiles each entry point whole, sees what the editor's single document"
    " cannot.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0212] A comment opening with `///` or `//!`, which is not read\n"
    "A documentation comment written in another language's style is ignored by KSPL. A line comment o"
    "pening with `///` (before a declaration or a field, or behind code) is the documentation marker "
    "of Rust, Swift, C#, Zig and Doxygen. In every one of them it describes the thing *after* it. `//"
    "!` is Rust's and Zig's marker for the thing it stands *inside* (at a file's head, the file). KSP"
    "L's marker is `//<`, behind the thing it describes. So neither reaches the editor's hover or the"
    " reference pages. Meanwhile, whoever wrote it believes the description took effect.\n"
    "* **Fix**: move the sentence behind the thing it describes, on that thing's own line, as `//<` ("
    "`fn add(a: I32, b: I32): I32 //< adds two numbers`). A longer description runs on below in `//<`"
    " lines one step in. A `//!` saying what a file is for becomes the `//<` run before the file's fi"
    "rst line of code, above the imports. A remark for whoever edits the code, rather than a descript"
    "ion for its callers, is a plain `//`.\n"
    "* **Not reported**:\n"
    "  * four or more slashes (a banner, as in Rust);\n"
    "  * a mark inside a string;\n"
    "  * a shebang. The analysis reads its `#!` rewritten to `//`, so followed by its path it would r"
    "ead `///`.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0213] A `//<` summary opening with a label\n"
    "The summary is the first line of a `//<` run, and the detail starts on the line below it. It is "
    "what the editor's hover, the completion list and `ksplc doc` show, so it is **one line that says"
    " what the thing is**. One opening with `Caution:` or `Note:` tells the reader what to beware of "
    "before it says what the thing is.\n"
    "* **Fix**: write a line above the label saying what the thing is. The label then opens the detai"
    "l.\n"
    "* **Not reported**: a label further down the run, in the detail.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0214] A test that no suite runs\n"
    "A top-level function's name starts with `test_`, but no suite in the program runs it, so it neve"
    "r runs and every check stays green. A suite (`impl S: suite.Suite`) runs the tests of its own fi"
    "le and of the files that file imports (\"8.5 Tests: functions named `test_`, run by a suite\" in"
    " [`../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md`](../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md)). The test"
    "'s file is part of the program, then, but it is imported only by files that hold no suite.\n"
    "* **Fix**: import the test's file from the file that holds the suite, or move the test into a fi"
    "le that file imports.\n"
    "* **Not reported**: a compile with no `main`. A file compiled alone (a library, an editor's view"
    " of one file) is not the program that runs its tests.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0501] Conformance to a look-alike of a known trait\n"
    "A type claims a trait that only looks like one the compiler treats specially, so the special tre"
    "atment never happens. The `X` of `impl <type>: X` names a different declaration with the same na"
    "me as a trait the compiler knows (`Ref_counted` / `Move_only` / `Manual_free` / `Drop`). The kno"
    "wn traits are matched by FQN, the name with its full module path (`std.lang.Ref_counted` and so "
    "on). So conforming to the look-alike turns on none of ARC's `retain`/`release`, automatic cleanu"
    "p, move checks or free tracking. **Nothing else reports this for these four.**\n"
    "* **Fix**: to conform to the real trait, qualify it with `lang.`, as in `impl <type>: lang.Ref_c"
    "ounted` (the `std/lang` item in [\"9. Files and import\" in `../../docs/SPEC-programming-languag"
    "e.md`](../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md#9-files-and-import)). Where the name serves an un"
    "related purpose, rename it, or silence this for that one file with `##lint(W0501 = off)`.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0502] A local or parameter hiding an imported module\n"
    "A local variable or parameter has the name an import line binds to a module (`import \"std/io\"`"
    " binds `io`, and `import \"std/json\" + { Value }` binds `json` beside `Value`). A module's memb"
    "ers and a value's are both reached with `.`, so from the declaration to the end of its scope `io"
    ".out()` reads the local. It then fails as a missing member far from the cause. Where the local h"
    "as a member of that name, it silently reads the wrong thing. It is a warning, not an error, beca"
    "use shadowing stays legal (\"4.5 Scope and shadowing\" in [`../../docs/SPEC-programming-language"
    ".md`](../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md)).\n"
    "* **Fix**: rename the local. The file can use the module only for names a selective import could"
    " bring (a type in signatures, `json.Value`). Then import those names instead (`import \"std/json"
    "\" { Value }`), so no module name is bound for a local to hide.\n"
    "* **Not reported**:\n"
    "  * a name no import line binds (a selective import without `+` binds only the names it lists);\n"
    "  * the prelude's `lang`, which no line imports;\n"
    "  * a name starting with `_`.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0503] An uncalled function in a file of tests\n"
    "A top-level function stands in a file of tests, nothing calls it, and its name does not start wi"
    "th `test_`. A suite finds a test by its name (\"8.5 Tests: functions named `test_`, run by a sui"
    "te\" in [`../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md`](../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md)). S"
    "o a test with a misspelt name (`tset_sum`) never runs, and this is the one sign of it. A file of"
    " tests is the suite's own file, or a file the suite's file imports that holds a test.\n"
    "* **Fix**: if it is a test, give it a name that starts with `test_`. Otherwise call it, or remov"
    "e it.\n"
    "* **Not reported**: a method, `main`, a name that starts with `_`, a function the compiler made,"
    " and a compile with no `main`.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [W0601] The use of an ARC-managed type\n"
    "Off by default (\"5.3\" in [`SPEC-tools.md`](SPEC-tools.md#53-enabling-disabling-or-promoting-a-"
    "diagnostic-to-an-error-lint----lint--ksplccfg)). ARC (automatic reference counting) has the comp"
    "iler insert `retain()`/`release()` calls that count a value's owners. They run invisibly, a risk"
    " where execution timing must stay visible. It is reported where a local variable's or parameter'"
    "s type is ARC-managed (conforms to `Ref_counted`). It is also reported where that type is a stru"
    "ct holding one as a value field at any depth.\n"
    "* **Fix**: consider explicit ownership through an arena or `defer`. It is not there to forbid AR"
    "C: a deliberate exception can stay. To exclude one file while it is enabled, write `##lint(W0601"
    " = off);`.\n"
    "\n"
    "## 3. The list of information diagnostics (I)\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0301] An unnecessary `::` cast\n"
    "A `::` cast (KSPL's explicit type conversion) that the compiler would do anyway. It is one of th"
    "ese:\n"
    "* a cast to the same type (`I32` to `I32`);\n"
    "* a lossless numeric widening (`I8` to `I32`);\n"
    "* a cast that only drops the write permission `$` (`U8$[]` to `U8[]`, `U8$@` to `U8@`).\n"
    "\n"
    "* **Fix**: drop the `::`; the conversion happens without it (the implicit casts in \"6.4 Type co"
    "nversion (`::`) and implicit casts\" in [`../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md`](../../docs/S"
    "PEC-programming-language.md)).\n"
    "* **The verdict is made against the expected type.** It is reported only where the surroundings "
    "fix the type (an argument, a return value, an annotated binding). It is not reported where the c"
    "ast is what fixes the binding's type, as in `x: = U8[]::f();`.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0302] A redundant boolean comparison\n"
    "A comparison with a boolean literal, as in `is_open == true` or `is_valid != false`.\n"
    "* **Fix**: evaluate it directly, as simply `is_open` or `!is_valid`.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0303] A redundant logical operation\n"
    "A logical operation with a boolean literal on one side, as in `a && true` or `b || false`.\n"
    "* **Fix**: the result is always that of `a` or `b` itself, so drop the operation.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0304] A redundant default case\n"
    "A `switch` over an enum has a `default` clause although `case`s cover every variant.\n"
    "* **Fix**: delete the `default`. Left in, it would hide a variant added later, which would go to"
    " it silently.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0305] A redundant `return;`\n"
    "A `return;` at the end of a `Void` function.\n"
    "* **Fix**: KSPL supplies it implicitly; delete it.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0306] A redundant continue\n"
    "A `continue;` as the last statement of a loop body.\n"
    "* **Fix**: the loop moves to the next iteration at its end anyway; delete it.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0307] A meaningless empty block\n"
    "An empty `stmt_block` (a block statement): a `defer {}`, an `if {}` with no `else`, an empty `el"
    "se {}`, an empty `for { }` and so on. One check gives the fix that fits the context (`defer`/`if"
    "`/`else`/`for`).\n"
    "* **Fix**: deleting it usually suffices. Where only the `if`'s `then` side is empty and the `els"
    "e` does the work, invert the condition with `!` and fold the `else` body into the `then` side. A"
    "n empty `for` can hang forever: add a body, or consider a break condition.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0308] A redundant if-return\n"
    "A branch that could return a boolean directly, as in `if cond { return true; } else { return fal"
    "se; }`.\n"
    "* **Fix**: return the condition itself: `return cond;`.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0309] An unnecessary explicit `$`\n"
    "`.` (a field access or method call) dereferences a pointer receiver automatically (follows the p"
    "ointer to the value), so an explicit `$` just before it is redundant (`(p$).field`, `p$.method()"
    "`). The `.` strips every level of a multiple pointer (`T@@` and the like).\n"
    "* **Fix**: delete the `$` and let `.` dereference (`p.field`).\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0310] An import's `as` that changes no name\n"
    "The `as io` of `import \"std/io\" as io` adds nothing: the bare `import \"std/io\"` derives the "
    "same alias from the path's last part. The two are entirely synonymous: two ways to write one thi"
    "ng.\n"
    "* **Fix**: drop the `as`: `import \"std/io\"`. The `as` itself is kept for imports that change t"
    "he name (`import \"ksplc/base/ast\" as base_ast`, or `import \"std/strfmt\" as _strfmt`, whose l"
    "eading `_` lets it go unused). Confined to them, it leaves one spelling per import.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0311] A free of a value that frees itself\n"
    "A local whose type frees itself (`Drop`) is freed by hand just where its own cleanup would run. "
    "Deleting the line changes nothing: the cleanup runs at the same point, on every way out, a propa"
    "gated failure's included. Three shapes are reported:\n"
    "* `defer x.free()` in the block that declares `x`;\n"
    "* `x.free()` as the last statement of the block that declares `x`;\n"
    "* `x.free()` right before a `return` or `throw` whose value calls nothing.\n"
    "\n"
    "A parameter taken by value counts as declared in the function's body.\n"
    "* **Not reported**: a free with more work after it in the scope. It releases early, where that w"
    "ork needs the resource gone. Examples are a lock let go before another process takes it, and a f"
    "ile closed before it is read back. A free before a `return` that still calls something is such a"
    " release too.\n"
    "* **Fix**: delete the line. Where the resource must go sooner, keep the free and put the work th"
    "at needs it gone after it.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0312] A `lang.` that changes no name\n"
    "A name is written after `lang.`, but the file can write it alone and mean the same declaration. "
    "`std/lang` enters every file: its alias `lang`, and each name it publishes with `pub` (\"9.2 Whi"
    "ch names an import brings in\" in [`../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md`](../../docs/SPEC-pr"
    "ogramming-language.md)). So `lang.Option` and `Option` would be two spellings of one name.\n"
    "* **Fix**: drop the `lang.`.\n"
    "* **Not reported**, since there the bare name is blocked:\n"
    "  * a name the file declares itself, or a written import brings in (`lang.Ref_counted` beside a "
    "`Ref_counted` of your own);\n"
    "  * a name a parameter or a local of the enclosing function takes;\n"
    "  * a declaration of `std/lang` without `pub`. Inside `std/` it is visible, but only `lang.` rea"
    "ches it: the implicit import brings in what `std/lang` publishes, and nothing else.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0313] A payload that nothing reads\n"
    "A `case` binds a variant's payload, but the case reads none of the names it binds (`case .add(_a"
    ", _b)`). A name that starts with `_` is a binding nobody reads, so it is reported too: to a read"
    "er it looks like an extraction. The rule is \"Write the payload only when it is needed\" in [`.."
    "/../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md`](../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md).\n"
    "* **Fix**: write the variant alone (`case .add`). The switch still checks that every variant is "
    "handled. Variants with payloads of different shapes fold into one `case` with commas.\n"
    "* **Not reported**:\n"
    "  * a payload one of whose names is read (`case .size(axis, _n)`);\n"
    "  * a payload holding a value it matches;\n"
    "  * the binding the compiler writes for `if v: = …` and for a `for` loop. [W0207] reports such"
    " a name where nothing reads it.\n"
    "\n"
    "  [W0207] does not report a name of a payload this reports.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0401] An unused import\n"
    "Something an `import` brought in is never used in that file. It comes in two shapes with one fix"
    ": a whole file's alias (`import \"path\" as name`) or one symbol of a selective import (`import "
    "\"path\" { A, B }`, the selective import of `docs/SPEC-programming-language.md` §9).\n"
    "* **The verdict covers that one file alone.** It rests only on whether the file writes the name,"
    " so a use inside a generic template never instantiated counts.\n"
    "* An alias counts only at the head of a qualified name (`io.out()`, `ds.List`), not as a field ("
    "`p.io`) or a function (`io()`) spelled the same.\n"
    "* An import an extension method's call needs (\"An extension method requires the import\" in [§"
    "9](../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md)) counts as used. This holds for a method on a built-"
    "in type too (`import \"std/str\"` for `s.equals(...)`) and for a format specifier (`import \"std"
    "/strfmt\"` for `\\{n:x\\}`).\n"
    "* **Fix**: delete the import. Where loading it is deliberate, mark it ignored: a `_` on the alia"
    "s, or a `_` leading the local name for a selective one (`{ A as _a }` and the like).\n"
    "  * A file can be imported only so that its conformances hold (`import \"std/strfmt\" as _strfmt"
    "` for the `fmt` each type's `\\{x\\}` reaches). The importing file writes nothing that uses it: "
    "the call is made inside `put`'s instantiation, which asks for no import. Give it the `_`.\n"
    "  * A name inside a declaration `#cfg` dropped is not counted, since that build lacks the declar"
    "ation. For a symbol used in only one of the builds, put the same `#cfg` on the import, **on the "
    "line directly before it**. Written on the same line, it is moved to its own line by `ksplc fmt` "
    "anyway.\n"
    "\n"
    "```kspl\n"
    "#cfg(freestanding)\n"
    "import \"std/sys\" { panic }\n"
    "```\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0402] A `pub` used only from inside its package\n"
    "Off by default (\"5.3\" in [`SPEC-tools.md`](SPEC-tools.md#53-enabling-disabling-or-promoting-a-"
    "diagnostic-to-an-error-lint----lint--ksplccfg)). A `pub` symbol is referred to only from inside "
    "its package, so it need not be public. **Only the entry file's package is examined**; another un"
    "it (`std/` and the like) is out of scope. A `struct`'s fields are never reported (they sit in an"
    "other table); top-level declarations and an `impl`'s methods (named `Type.method`) are.\n"
    "* **Fix**: remove the `pub`, leaving the visibility unset.\n"
    "  * Caution: **Do not enable it on a unit handed out as a library.** It sees only the references"
    " in that compilation, so it calls a `pub` with no outside users yet unneeded, though that `pub` "
    "is the published API itself. Use it where the unit is closed, as for an executable.\n"
    "  * A trait method's `pub` inherits the trait's own visibility, so removing it changes nothing ("
    "it need not be fixed even where reported).\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0501] Shadowing\n"
    "An inner block scope redeclares a variable an outer scope already defines.\n"
    "* **Fix**: shadowing is legal in KSPL. But to avoid declaring a new variable while meaning to up"
    "date the outer one, prefer a different name (`inner_x`) wherever possible.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0502] A naming-convention violation\n"
    "An identifier breaks KSPL's standard naming conventions.\n"
    "* **Fix**: rename it to follow these:\n"
    "\n"
    "  | The kind of name | The convention |\n"
    "  | :-- | :-- |\n"
    "  | a type, a struct, an enum, a typedef | `Initial_upper_snake_case`: only the first letter is "
    "uppercase; all later letters are lowercase |\n"
    "  | an explicit constant (`const`) | `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`: all letters are uppercase |\n"
    "  | an ordinary binding, a function, a method, a field, an enum variant | `snake_case`: all lett"
    "ers are lowercase |\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0503] A function of one enum outside its `impl`\n"
    "A top-level function takes, as its first parameter, an enum declared in the same file, by value."
    " Written as the enum's method, what the enum does stands where the enum is. The call then reads "
    "in the order the value flows (`state.text()`, not `state_text(state)`).\n"
    "* **Fix**: move it into the enum's `impl` as a method that takes `Self` (`fn text(r: Self): U8[]"
    "`), and call it as `x.text()`.\n"
    "* **Not reported**:\n"
    "  * an enum of another file or package. Its `impl` would hold another package's reading of it;\n"
    "  * a pointer to the enum (`State@`), a slice of it, or the enum taken second;\n"
    "  * a generic function, a function C calls (`#export`), and a method already in an `impl`.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0601] An unnecessary mutability marker\n"
    "A variable or parameter declared with the mutability marker `$` is never reassigned within its s"
    "cope (Rust's `unused_mut`). A declaration that cannot take a `$`, such as a for-in loop's variab"
    "le (`for x in collection { ... }`), is out of scope.\n"
    "* **The criterion**: whether the value the name itself holds is rewritten (`is_reassigned` in `s"
    "ema/flow/track.kspl`). A write that goes through a pointer or a slice to something else does not"
    " count.\n"
    "* **Why a write can need no `$` before the name**: KSPL has two places to write `$`, and they al"
    "low different things. I0601 asks only about the first. The full rule is \"4.2 The two axes of mu"
    "tability\" in [`../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md`](../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.m"
    "d#42-the-two-axes-of-mutability).\n"
    "\n"
    "  | Where the `$` stands | Example | What it allows |\n"
    "  | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n"
    "  | Before the name | `$p: I32@ = a@` | Putting another value into `p` (`p = b@`) |\n"
    "  | Inside the type | `p: I32$@ = a@` | Writing to what `p` points at (`p$ = 5`), while `p` itse"
    "lf stays fixed |\n"
    "\n"
    "  So a write needs the `$` before the name only where it changes the value the name holds. A str"
    "uct's fields and a fixed-length array's elements are part of that value. What a pointer points a"
    "t, and a slice's elements, are not: they live elsewhere, and the type's `$` (`T$@`, `T$[]`) allo"
    "ws writing them.\n"
    "\n"
    "  | The write | Is a `$` before the name needed | Why |\n"
    "  | :-- | :-- | :-- |\n"
    "  | `p = other;` | **yes** | The name is given another value |\n"
    "  | `p.f = v` / `p$ = v`, `p` a pointer | no | What `p` points at changes, not `p`; `p`'s type `"
    "T$@` allows it |\n"
    "  | `s[i] = v`, `s` a slice | no | An element changes, not `s`; `s`'s type `T$[]` allows it |\n"
    "  | `v.f = v2`, `v` a struct held **by value** (not through a pointer) | **yes** | The field is "
    "part of `v`'s own value |\n"
    "  | `s.ptr = p`, `s` a slice | **yes** | A slice's own value is its pointer and length (its desc"
    "riptor), so this rewrites `s` itself |\n"
    "  | `v.m()`, `v` held **by value** and `m` taking `Self$@` | **yes** | The method can rewrite `v"
    "`'s own value, as `v.f = v2` does |\n"
    "  | `p.m()`, `p` a pointer or a slice | no | The method rewrites what `p` points at |\n"
    "  | `v.m()`, `m` taking `Self@` or `Self` | no | The method only reads `v` |\n"
    "  | `p$` / `s[i]` / `s[i].m()` read or called, `p` a pointer and `s` a slice | no | Nothing of t"
    "he name's own value is written |\n"
    "  | `a[i] = v`, `a` a **fixed-length array** (`I32[4]`) | **yes** | The elements are part of the"
    " array's own value |\n"
    "  | `x@` (taking an address) | **yes** | `x@` is a pointer that can write (`T$@`) only when `x` "
    "has the `$`, so the `$` decides what the pointer can do |\n"
    "  | `U8$[]::buf` (a writable slice made from the array `buf`) | **yes** | Without the `$`, `buf`"
    " becomes read-only memory in the generated C, and a write through the slice would hit it |\n"
    "* **Fix**: remove the `$` (`$x: = ...` → `x: = ...`). Unmarked is immutable, so the type syste"
    "m then guarantees the variable is never reassigned.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0602] Detecting a hand-made ownership struct\n"
    "Off by default (\"5.3\" in [`SPEC-tools.md`](SPEC-tools.md#53-enabling-disabling-or-promoting-a-"
    "diagnostic-to-an-error-lint----lint--ksplccfg)). Reported where a struct not conforming to `Ref_"
    "counted` holds a raw-pointer field and defines its own `free` method: hand-made ownership. Struc"
    "ts under `std/` are out of scope (ARC's own primitives, such as `Arena` and `List`, would make t"
    "he advice circular).\n"
    "* **Fix**: following `Rc<|T|>` / `Arc<|T|>`, consider conforming it to `Ref_counted` so that ref"
    "erence counting manages its lifetime (\"8.4 Memory management\" in [`../../docs/SPEC-programming"
    "-language.md`](../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md)).\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0701] Error propagation using '!' is recommended\n"
    "A `catch` that only rethrows the caught error, as in `catch e { throw e; }`.\n"
    "* **Fix**: replace the whole `catch` block with the `!` operator, which propagates the error (ha"
    "nds it to the caller) more safely and concisely.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [I0702] An arena parameter with a default\n"
    "A parameter that takes an arena has a default (`a: Arena$@ = null`). A call that leaves the argu"
    "ment out does not show where it allocates, nor whether the caller owes a `free()`.\n"
    "* **Fix**: remove the default. A caller that wants the global heap passes `null` (`List<|I32|>.n"
    "ew(null)`).\n"
    "* The type is read as the analysis resolved it. So an arena behind a typedef, or behind a pointe"
    "r of either kind (`Arena@`, `Arena$@`), is found.\n"
    "\n"
    "## 4. The list of hints (H)\n"
    "\n"
    "### [H0301] Unnecessary brackets\n"
    "Brackets `()` that change nothing. They stand in one of two places:\n"
    "* around the condition of an `if`, a `for`, a `switch` and the like;\n"
    "* around the receiver of a postfix operator (`.`/`[`/`(`/`@`/`$`/`::`/`!`) that binds the same w"
    "ithout them. Such a receiver is an identifier, a path, a literal, an existing postfix chain and "
    "so on (`(fc1_lora$).forward(x)`, `(r::U8).is_space()`).\n"
    "\n"
    "* **Fix**: delete the brackets. Some expressions bind differently without them: a binary, unary "
    "or ternary operation, a `catch`, a `sizeof`, an upcast. They are out of scope, since there the b"
    "rackets are needed.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [H0302] A type written twice\n"
    "A type written where the line, or the place the value goes, already says it. It has two shapes, "
    "each with its own message.\n"
    "\n"
    "* **A local's annotation that its initializer already says.** The same type is written at the in"
    "itializer's head. It is written as one of these:\n"
    "  * a struct literal (`p: Point = Point::{ … }`);\n"
    "  * a cast (`n: U8 = U8::v`);\n"
    "  * an enum variant (`k: Kind = Kind.small`);\n"
    "  * a call made through the type (`xs: List<|I32|> = List<|I32|>.new(arena)`).\n"
    "\n"
    "  The annotation can also be `Bool` over `true` / `false`. Deleting it leaves the variable's typ"
    "e as it is. **Only a type the text shows is reported.** `count: Sz = items.len` is not reported:"
    " there the annotation is the one place a reader without hover sees the type.\n"
    "* **An enum's type written at its variant where the place already gives it.** The places are the"
    " nine where the type name can be left out (\"6.6.2 Where the type may be omitted\" in [`../../do"
    "cs/SPEC-programming-language.md`](../../docs/SPEC-programming-language.md)):\n"
    "  1. the right side of a declaration with a written type (`const USUAL: Kind = Kind.small`);\n"
    "  2. the right side of `=` (`held = Option<|I32|>.some(3)`);\n"
    "  3. a direct argument of a call (`kinds.push(Kind.small)`, a call through a function value's to"
    "o);\n"
    "  4. a variant's payload (`.some(Kind.small)`);\n"
    "  5. a struct literal's field (`Slot::{ .kind = Kind.small }`);\n"
    "  6. an array literal's element (`::{ Kind.small, Kind.big(3) }`);\n"
    "  7. what a `catch` stands in for (`sized(n) catch Kind.small`, or after `fallback`);\n"
    "  8. the right side of `return` (`return Option<|I32|>.some(n)` in a function returning `I32\?`)"
    ";\n"
    "  9. the right side of `throw` (`throw Fault.lost`).\n"
    "\n"
    "  In each, either side of an `if … else` standing there counts too (`return Option<|I32|>.some"
    "(n) if ok else Option<|I32|>.none`). The variant's type is the one the place expects: a `T!E` fu"
    "nction's `T` for a `return`, its `E` for a `throw`. So `.small` finds the same variant and the p"
    "rogram does not change.\n"
    "* **Fix**: delete the type the editor dims. That is the annotation (`p: = Point::{ … }`; a `co"
    "nst` loses its `:` with it, `const K = Kind.small`). Or it is the variant's type up to its `.` ("
    "`kinds.push(.small)`, `return .some(n)`, `throw .lost`, `catch .small`).\n"
    "* **Not reported**:\n"
    "  * an annotation doing work: a literal taking its type from it (`$i: Sz = 0`), a conversion it "
    "drives (`wide: I64 = small`), or a right side typed by it (`::{ … }`, `.small`);\n"
    "  * another name for the type (`spot: Spot = Point::{ … }`, `Spot` a typedef);\n"
    "  * a cast [I0301] calls unnecessary, which is left to [I0301]. Deleting both would leave the va"
    "lue's own type;\n"
    "  * a call through a type inside a generic (`b: Box<|T|> = Box<|T|>.make(v)`).\n"
    "\n"
    "  The compiler checks a generic's body once per instantiation (once per set of type arguments it"
    " is used with). An `impl` written for one instantiation alone (`impl Box<|I32|>`) can give `make"
    "` another return type there. So the annotation can repeat the type for one instantiation and be "
    "needed for another. The advice, however, is about the one line both share. The other shapes name"
    " their type in the text, which is the same for every instantiation. So they are reported, once p"
    "er line however many instantiations check it.\n"
    "* **Not reported at a variant**:\n"
    "  * a declaration whose annotation is reported (`k: Kind = Kind.small` loses the annotation, so "
    "the line has one fix);\n"
    "  * every place outside the nine, where the name stays.\n"
    "\n"
    "  The places outside the nine are an inferred declaration (`k: = Kind.small`) and an argument a "
    "generic infers its type from (`same(Kind.small)`). They are also a comparison (`mode == Mode.on`"
    "), either side of an `if … else` standing outside the nine, and a compound assignment such as "
    "`+=`. A struct literal keeps its type at these places too (`return Point::{ … }`).\n"
    "\n"
    "### [H0701] `T\?` proposed for a raw pointer that can be null\n"
    "A function returning a raw pointer (`T@`) can hand back null on some path, so a caller can forge"
    "t to check for null. Three shapes are reported:\n"
    "* `return null`;\n"
    "* a `return` of an `if … else` with a `null` side (`return null if c else p`);\n"
    "* a `return` of a local the function declares or assigns as `null` anywhere (a list's head kept "
    "null until the first push).\n"
    "\n"
    "The last is not followed through the flow. So a local set to null and then filled on every path "
    "is reported too. It is on by default, whatever is being built (\"5.3\" in [`SPEC-tools.md`](SPEC"
    "-tools.md#53-enabling-disabling-or-promoting-a-diagnostic-to-an-error-lint----lint--ksplccfg)). "
    "Out of scope:\n"
    "* `std/mem` and `std/libc` (memory and C, whose contract is a raw null);\n"
    "* a function with `#export`. That is the FFI boundary with C, where `T\?` is not ABI-compatible:"
    " C cannot read its memory layout.\n"
    "\n"
    "* **Not reported**:\n"
    "  * a local whose address is lent (`fill(p@)`), since what is written through the address goes u"
    "nseen;\n"
    "  * a `return` after a call declared `#noreturn` in the same block, which is never reached. It i"
    "s the `return` an internal-error stop still needs.\n"
    "* **Fix**: return `T\?` instead, as `.some(value)` / `.none`. The `switch`'s exhaustiveness chec"
    "k then stops callers from forgetting to handle the absent value.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [H0801] A guard clause is recommended\n"
    "An `if` block always leaves, so control never reaches the code after the `if` from it, and an `e"
    "lse` block follows. It leaves by one of these:\n"
    "* a `return`, a `throw` or a `fallback`;\n"
    "* a `break` or `continue` of a loop or of an enclosing label;\n"
    "* a call declared not to return (`panic`);\n"
    "* a `for` that never ends;\n"
    "* an inner `if` or `switch` all of whose branches leave.\n"
    "\n"
    "* **Not reported**: an `if` that a `break` to its own label leaves (`ui: if c`). `break ui` cont"
    "inues after the `if`, so without the `else` its body would run after the `break` as well, and a "
    "`break ui` inside the `else` would be left with no label.\n"
    "* **Fix**: the code after the `if` already acts as the `else`. Remove the `else` to reduce the i"
    "ndentation, into the \"reject first and leave\" shape.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [H0802] A wrap at a call inside an argument\n"
    "A call is passed as an argument to another call, and the outer argument list wraps over several "
    "lines, tangling with the inner call's wrapping (`borrows.push(base_core.arena.create(Lock_borrow"
    "::{ .borrow = id.text,\\n    .guard = g, ... }))`). **The inner call's own argument list must wr"
    "ap too.** Where only the outer one wraps, the inner call stays on one line as a single argument "
    "and does not tangle. Only the argument list's own wrapping is judged (as with [H0804]), so a cha"
    "ined call at a method boundary is out of scope.\n"
    "* **Fix**: move the inner call into a named temporary and pass that (`lb: = base_core.arena.crea"
    "te(Lock_borrow::{ ... }); borrows.push(lb);`).\n"
    "\n"
    "### [H0803] A function exceeds 300 lines\n"
    "A function or method exceeds 300 lines. The limit is defined in \"Principle 3 - what is counted,"
    " and how\" in [`../../docs/SPEC-gates.md`](../../docs/SPEC-gates.md).\n"
    "* **Fix**: split it into small helpers by task.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [H0804] A direct wrap of a multi-line call\n"
    "The subject of a `switch` or `if` directly wraps a call whose argument list spans several lines "
    "(`switch x.foo(a, b,\\n    c) case ...`). The wrapping tangles with the `switch`/`case` or the `"
    "if`'s condition and is hard to read. Out of scope: a chained call wrapping between the receiver "
    "and the method name (`x.method()\\n    .chain()`), and a single struct literal as the argument.\n"
    "* **Fix**: assign the call to a temporary and make that the subject (`result: = x.foo(a, b, c); "
    "switch result case ...`); the call itself can still wrap.\n"
    "* It urges splitting for readability, not correctness, so it is a Hint, like H0802, which gives "
    "the same advice.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [H0805] A function nests blocks past 8 levels\n"
    "A function's blocks nest deeper than 8 levels. The limit is defined in \"Principle 3 - what is c"
    "ounted, and how\" in [`../../docs/SPEC-gates.md`](../../docs/SPEC-gates.md). The function body i"
    "s level 0, so each `if`, `for`, `switch` case or bare block inside it opens one more. It is repo"
    "rted once per function, at the function, with the depth it reached.\n"
    "* **Fix**: move the innermost part into a helper, or reject first and leave ([H0801]) so the res"
    "t needs no `else`.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [H0806] A comment past its cap\n"
    "A comment holds more lines before the next line of code than its cap: 6 lines, or 10 for the hea"
    "der at a file's start. The caps are \"Comment notation and limits\" in [`../../docs/SPEC-plain-l"
    "anguage.md`](../../docs/SPEC-plain-language.md). A long comment holds background that a design d"
    "ocument is the place for.\n"
    "* **What counts**: every comment line up to the next line of code. **A blank line ends nothing**"
    ", or one in the middle would let a comment of any length pass.\n"
    "* **What does not count**:\n"
    "  * the summary of a `//<` run, its first line with text;\n"
    "  * a line a gate reads, such as `// EXPECT:` (an upper-case name of three letters or more, then"
    " a colon);\n"
    "  * a line inside a string.\n"
    "* **What ends a comment early**: a section divider (a comment whose text opens with `---` or `=="
    "=`) ends the comment it stands in. An empty `//<` parts two paragraphs of a run's detail, and th"
    "e count starts again after it.\n"
    "* **The header**: the first comment past 6 lines before any declaration takes the cap of 10. An "
    "`import` line and a `#!` line are no declaration.\n"
    "* **Fix**: shorten the comment, or move the background to the design document of its subject and"
    " point at it.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [H0807] A `//<` run below a `{}`\n"
    "A `//<` run goes on below a line whose code ends in `{}` (`impl Row: Move_only {} //< …`). The"
    " run then stands outside the declaration it describes. A declaration without braces (a field, a "
    "constant, a `typedef`) keeps its run below it, indented, and is not reported.\n"
    "* **Fix**: open the braces, and put the `//<` lines between them.\n"
    "\n"
    "### [H0808] A file's description below a banner\n"
    "A file describes itself in a `//<` run before its first line of code, but another comment stands"
    " above that run. A reader's eye lands on the first line, and a banner there pushes what the file"
    " is for off the screen. A `#!` line is not counted as a banner.\n"
    "* **Fix**: move the `//<` run to the top of the file, and put the other comment below it.\n"
    "";
